/// \defgroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar Window event handling: gadget input, message box, radar input, window layouts and control bar schemes
/// \ingroup grp_gui_apt
/// \brief The callbacks through which legacy windows react to the mouse and
///        keyboard (push button, list boxes, combo box, radar window,
///        message box), the list box scroll bar factory, the window layout
///        list, the control bar scheme manager's start-up and the control
///        bar's multi-select command merging.
///
/// This group is the event-handling side of the legacy window system.
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets, \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api and
/// \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists describe the gadget API that screens call to
/// build and fill gadgets; this group describes what happens when the
/// player presses a key or clicks one.
///
/// \par Input and system callbacks
/// Every GameWindow carries two callbacks with the same shape: they take the
/// window, a message number and two data words, and return a
/// WindowMsgHandledType. MSG_HANDLED means the window consumed the message;
/// MSG_IGNORED lets the window manager offer it to another window. An
/// \e input callback receives raw device messages (GWM_LEFT_DOWN,
/// GWM_LEFT_UP, GWM_CHAR, GWM_MOUSE_POS, GWM_WHEEL_UP and the like). A
/// \e system callback receives lifecycle messages (create, destroy, resize,
/// focus) and the notification messages that a gadget's children and the
/// gadget itself send. The names used here are Zero Hour's; BFME 2 numbers
/// some gadget messages differently (see
/// \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api).
///
/// A gadget turns raw input into notifications for its \e owner window,
/// usually the screen that created it: a push button sends GBM_SELECTED, a
/// list box GLM_SELECTED, GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED or GLM_RIGHT_CLICKED, and a
/// combo box GCM_SELECTED. The owner's system callback acts on them. The
/// notification goes through GameWindowManager::winSendSystemMsg, which
/// runs the receiver's callback before it returns, so data words that point
/// at locals are valid for the whole call.
///
/// In BFME 2 the gadget callbacks are mostly called from the virtual input
/// and system methods of small per-gadget GameWindow subclasses that live next
/// to the W3D draw code (see \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets); Zero Hour stored the
/// callbacks as function pointers on the window instead. A BFME 2 window can
/// still carry an input callback pointer, which is how LeftHUDInput() is
/// attached to the radar window. Windows made from .wnd layout files reach
/// callbacks by name through the function lexicon
/// (\ref grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd).
///
/// \par What is here
/// - GadgetPushButtonInput(): clicks, auto-repeat, keyboard activation and
///   the click sound of push buttons (and so of check-like buttons).
/// - GadgetListBoxInput() and GadgetListBoxMultiInput(): row selection,
///   scrolling and key handling of single-select and multi-select list
///   boxes. getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord() is the hit test they use for
///   mouse-over row tracking; their click handling repeats the same walk
///   inline. GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar() builds a list box's scroll bar.
/// - GadgetComboBoxSystem(): the combo box's message handler.
/// - MessageBoxSystem(): the window-based message box's button handling.
/// - LeftHUDInput(): clicks on the radar (minimap) window.
/// - WindowLayout: the list of top-level windows that one layout file
///   creates, with WindowLayout::addWindow, WindowLayout::removeWindow and
///   WindowLayout::bringForward.
/// - ControlBarSchemeManager::init(), which loads the control bar skins,
///   and ControlBar::addCommonCommands and ControlBar::populateMultiSelect,
///   which build the command bar for a selection of several units.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Everything in this group is client-side window state. None of it runs
///   in the game logic, none of these bodies uses the logic random number
///   generator, and the control bar scheme INI files are outside the INI CRC,
///   so changing how a window reacts cannot desynchronise a lockstep game by
///   itself. The orders that radar clicks and command buttons issue do
///   reach the logic as GameMessages; those are the lockstep-relevant part
///   (see \ref page_core_frameworks).
/// - Data-driven: the .wnd layouts that choose which callback a window uses
///   (by name, from the function lexicon table that the keyword searches);
///   the control bar skins in ControlBarScheme.ini (ControlBarSchemeManager);
///   and the creation settings of list boxes and combo boxes, which a layout
///   gives in its LISTBOXDATA and COMBOBOXDATA blocks (see
///   GadgetListBoxInput() and GadgetComboBoxSystem()).
/// - Click sounds. A push button's click sound is not assigned by data. The
///   control bar's command buttons get their audio event names from code:
///   ControlBar::setControlCommand attaches GUIControlButtonClick to every
///   window it binds a command to, and ControlBar::populateSpecialPowerShortcut
///   then gives the special power shortcut buttons GUIGenShortcutClick
///   instead. Both are string literals in those bodies. Neither a .wnd file
///   nor the CommandButton INI block has a field for it: the script keyword
///   table has no push button data block, and the CommandButton sound keys
///   (UnitSpecificSound, SetAutoAbilityUnitSound and UnsetAutoAbilityUnitSound)
///   are not read by the bodies documented here (Zero Hour: UnitSpecificSound
///   is the voice a unit plays when it carries out the command; the BFME 2
///   readers were not traced). A mod can therefore redefine what those two audio events play
///   (the audio event definitions, \ref sub_audio_modding; the button keeps
///   the reference handle that an audio manager method returns for the event
///   name) but cannot choose a
///   different event for a button without a code change. List boxes and
///   combo boxes differ: their click sound reference is read from the audio
///   manager's misc-audio block, very probably the ComboBoxClick entry of
///   the MiscAudio INI block (see GadgetListBoxInput() for the evidence and
///   its limits).
/// - The CommandButton options field: its bits decide, among other things,
///   what the multi-select bar can show (ControlBar::addCommonCommands),
///   whether a command button is check-like (ControlBar::setControlCommand)
///   and whether a pending command needs a target position (LeftHUDInput).
///   The BFME 2 bodies test these bits by number. Any assignment of INI
///   option names to those bits is carried from Zero Hour and is not
///   confirmed for BFME 2. The name list recorded for the CommandButton
///   Options field is a shorter, differently ordered list that has no
///   NEED_TARGET_POS, while a longer option-name list recorded for another
///   module's Options field holds Zero Hour's three names for these bits at
///   the positions that match Zero Hour's assignment. Which list the
///   CommandButton field really uses is unresolved, so this page does not say
///   which INI option name sets each bit.
/// - Hard-coded: which named callbacks exist (a layout cannot add one), the
///   four button names of the message box, the audio event names of the
///   control bar's command buttons, the 150 ms repeat delay and the 21 by 22
///   pixel size (at 1024 by 768) of list box scroll bar buttons, the 32-slot
///   size of the command bar, and the key bindings of the push button and
///   list box callbacks documented here.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Timing uses wall-clock milliseconds from the Win32 multimedia timer
///   (timeGetTime) for button auto-repeat and list box double clicks, and
///   the list box double-click interval is a global that a start-up
///   initializer fills from the operating system's double-click time
///   (GetDoubleClickTime). None of it follows the logic
///   frame rate. A port needs a monotonic millisecond clock and must keep in
///   mind that these comparisons are written for 32-bit unsigned values.
/// - Mouse positions travel in one 32-bit data word, x in the low 16 bits
///   and y in the high 16 bits; message data words are 32 bits and several
///   messages pass pointers through them (a window, a string, a stack
///   record). A 64-bit port must widen the data words and the position
///   packing.
/// - Layout arithmetic is in whole pixels. Scroll bar buttons scale with
///   the resolution against a 1024 by 768 design (GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar()),
///   but their margins and the combo box list margins do not; control bar
///   skins scale by display size over the skin's creation resolution
///   (ControlBarSchemeManager).
/// - Sounds are raised through the audio manager (TheAudio) as audio
///   events, so a new audio back end only has to honour those events.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt, \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets,
///      \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api, \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists,
///      \ref grp_w1019_gui_named_callbacks_wnd


/// \class ControlBar
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The context-sensitive command bar of the in-game HUD: it owns the
///        command button windows and decides which commands they show for
///        the current selection.
///
/// ControlBar is Zero Hour's class of the same name, and TheControlBar is its
/// single instance. It is a client object: each machine builds its own from
/// the local player's selection. What a button press does reaches the logic
/// as GameMessages (\ref page_core_frameworks).
///
/// \par What it owns
/// - A fixed array of command windows, one per slot of a CommandSet. BFME 2
///   builds 32 of them from the ControlBar.wnd ButtonCommand template under
///   a full-screen command window (Zero Hour: 18 slots). Slot i of a command
///   set maps to command window i.
/// - The common-command table used for multi-selection: one CommandButton
///   pointer per slot, parallel to the window array (see
///   addCommonCommands() and populateMultiSelect()).
/// - The drawable whose commands are shown in the single-unit panels, and
///   the current context, which selects among the panels (single-unit
///   commands, structure inventory, beacon, construction, observer list and
///   others). ControlBar::switchToContext moves between contexts and calls
///   the matching populate routine; the multi-select context calls
///   populateMultiSelect(). ControlBar::update, ControlBar::reset and the
///   functions that pick a scheme for a player are among its callers.
/// - The ControlBarSchemeManager, which holds the skins and picks the one
///   for the local player's side.
///
/// \par Lifecycle
/// InGameUI::init calls InGameUI::createControlBar (Zero Hour: it creates the
/// ControlBar.wnd window), then makes the object with new and calls
/// ControlBar::init. ControlBar::init looks up the ControlBar.wnd windows
/// and creates the scheme manager (which reads the skin INI files).
/// InGameUI::recreateControlBar deletes the object and builds it again. A
/// display-mode change calls it (Zero Hour's options screen did; in BFME 2 the
/// pending-mode switch that GameClient::update runs refreshes the in-game UI
/// through the same virtual slot), so the layout, the command windows and the
/// skins are rebuilt for the new display size.
/// ControlBar::init is a subsystem initialiser in BFME 2 (the INI loads that
/// Zero Hour does inside it were replaced by the subsystem legend loader).
///
/// \par Command buttons and command sets
/// The commands themselves are data: CommandButton blocks define the
/// buttons and CommandSet blocks list each object's slots
/// (\ref page_core_frameworks_commands). ControlBar::setControlCommand binds
/// a CommandButton to a window. Every command window's state (hidden,
/// enabled, greyed) is recomputed from the selection. For one command type the
/// multi-select path also hands a computed index to a setter on the
/// CommandButton itself (addCommonCommands() documents it; what the index
/// selects is not established).
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: ControlBar.wnd (layout and window names), CommandButton and
///   CommandSet INI blocks, ControlBarScheme.ini (skins). Which commands
///   survive in a multi-selection is decided by one bit of the CommandButton
///   options field (see ControlBar::addCommonCommands); which INI option name
///   sets that bit in BFME 2 is not established.
/// - Fixed in code: 32 command slots, the set of contexts, and the window
///   names that ControlBar::init looks up (ControlBar.wnd:ControlBarParent,
///   ControlBar.wnd:CommandWindow, ControlBar.wnd:ButtonCommand and others),
///   so a replacement ControlBar.wnd has to define them. What happens when
///   one is missing is not established.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The bar is laid out from ControlBar.wnd at its authoring resolution and
/// rescaled by the window system; the skins add their own scaling
/// (ControlBarSchemeManager). A resolution change is handled by rebuilding
/// the whole bar rather than re-laying it out.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The role and the slot model are Zero Hour's (GeneralsMD ControlBar.h and
/// ControlBar.cpp). The context switch, the 32-slot size, the command window
/// construction and the subsystem-legend change are read from BFME 2's own
/// ControlBar::init and ControlBar::switchToContext bodies.
///
/// \see ControlBarSchemeManager, \ref sub_gui_apt_windows


/// \class ControlBarSchemeManager
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief Owns the control bar skins (schemes), picks the one for the local
///        player's side and display size, and draws and animates it.
///
/// A scheme (class ControlBarScheme) is a skin of the in-game control bar: a
/// set of images, colours and rectangles for the bar's chrome (the
/// right-hand HUD image, the options, idle worker, buddy, beacon, general
/// and similar buttons, the minimise toggle, the power bar and money areas),
/// plus layered decorative images and simple animations. The manager is the
/// owner of all of them.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// ControlBar::init creates the manager with new, keeps it for the life of
/// the bar and calls ControlBarSchemeManager::init. The manager owns every
/// scheme it parsed: its destructor deletes them all. Because the bar is
/// rebuilt after a resolution change (see ControlBar), the manager and its
/// schemes are re-read from the INI files at that point.
///
/// \par Where schemes come from
/// ControlBarSchemeManager::init reads the INI files. The ControlBarScheme
/// block parser, INI::parseControlBarSchemeDefinition, finds the manager
/// through TheControlBar, asks it for a scheme with the block's name
/// (ControlBarSchemeManager::newControlBarScheme) and fills it from the field
/// table. Scheme names are stored lower-cased and looked up without regard to
/// case. If a scheme of the same name already exists, it is reset and reused,
/// so the second file can replace a scheme from the first. The parser reads
/// the manager out of TheControlBar, which must therefore exist, and skips
/// the block if the bar holds no manager yet.
///
/// \par The scheme fields
/// BFME 2's field table (reverse/fieldparse_tables.tsv has the exact list)
/// includes: Side, ScreenCreationRes, QueueButtonImage, RightHUDImage,
/// BuildUpClockColor, CommandBarBorderColor, the ButtonBorder colours (Build,
/// Action, Upgrade, System and Altered), Enable/Hightlited/Pushed image sets
/// (and, for most of them, a Disabled image) for the Options, IdleWorker,
/// Buddy, Beacon, General, MinMax and UAttack buttons (the original
/// spellings, including "Hightlited"), the GenBarButton In/On images, the
/// ToggleButton Up and Down In/On/Pushed images, GenArrow, the upper-left and lower-right
/// rectangle pair for each control (MinMax, General, UAttack, Options,
/// Worker, Chat, Beacon, PowerBar, Money), CommandMarkerImage,
/// ExpBarForegroundImage, and the repeatable ImagePart and AnimatingPart
/// sub-blocks that add decorative layers. The Disabled image exists for
/// Options, IdleWorker, Buddy, Beacon and General but not for MinMax and
/// UAttack, and the GenBarButton and ToggleButton images use In/On (and
/// Pushed) names instead. Zero Hour's table has a PowerPurchaseImage key that BFME 2's
/// lacks, and BFME 2 adds ButtonBorderAlteredColor (a fifth button border
/// colour).
///
/// \par Choosing a scheme
/// - ControlBarSchemeManager::setControlBarSchemeByPlayer and
///   ControlBarSchemeManager::setControlBarSchemeByPlayerTemplate look at the
///   side name: the player's side for the first, the template's side for the
///   second. Only the by-template variant has an optional suffix: its
///   useSmall parameter, when true, appends Small to the template's side.
///   The by-player variant takes only a player and uses that player's side
///   unchanged. If the current scheme's side already equals the name, the
///   scheme is just re-initialised (this check comes first). Otherwise, in
///   both variants an empty side becomes Observer. Among the schemes whose
///   Side matches without regard to case, the one with the largest creation
///   width wins, whatever the actual display width is. If none matches, the
///   scheme named Default is used. When a scheme matched by side, the scale
///   factors are recomputed as the display size divided by that scheme's
///   ScreenCreationRes, as floating-point values, one per axis; the Default
///   fallback leaves them as they were.
/// - ControlBarSchemeManager::setControlBarSchemeByPlayer also rebinds the
///   bar's popup communicator button before it chooses a scheme: in a
///   multiplayer game the button gets the command button named
///   NonCommand_Communicator, otherwise the one named NonCommand_BriefingHistory.
///   The button is bound to whichever CommandButton carries that name; the
///   body shows no failure path if a name is missing: the lookup result is
///   passed on as it is, and ControlBar::setControlCommand handles a null
///   command button by clearing the button's image and text and returning
///   before it binds anything, so the button is simply left unbound.
/// - ControlBarSchemeManager::setControlBarScheme selects a scheme by name.
///   It computes the scale factors with integer division and stores them as
///   floats, so a display that is not an exact multiple of the creation
///   resolution gets a rounded-down whole-number factor. A name that is not
///   found leaves no current scheme.
/// - ControlBarSchemeManager::drawForeground and
///   ControlBarSchemeManager::drawBackground pass the scale factors and the
///   bar's offset to the current scheme, which draws its layers. They do
///   nothing without a current scheme.
/// - The per-frame step is separate. ControlBar::update calls a small
///   null-checked forward on the manager that passes on to the current
///   scheme's update, with no factors and no offset; that update steps each
///   of the scheme's animations. Nothing happens when there is no current scheme. Zero Hour has a single animation type, a slide to the
///   right; BFME 2's animation types were not checked.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: everything above lives in Data\INI\ControlBarScheme.ini
///   and Data\INI\Default\ControlBarScheme.ini (a mod ships an edited copy in
///   a BIG file or as a loose file, as for other INI data; see
///   \ref page_modding_files). The images are mapped images defined by the
///   image INI data.
/// - A mod that adds a faction needs a scheme whose Side equals the side name
///   its players use, or the Default scheme is used.
/// - The scheme INI files load without the CRC Xfer, so they are outside the
///   INI CRC (\ref sub_gui_apt_modding).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Scaling is non-uniform: separate x and y factors from display size over
///   the creation resolution, so a different aspect ratio stretches the bar.
///   The creation resolution picks only the largest authored skin; it does
///   not look at the real display size.
/// - Integer division in the by-name path means fractional factors are
///   truncated; the by-player paths keep fractions.
/// - Skin images are fixed bitmaps; a higher resolution scales them up.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The class is Zero Hour's ControlBarSchemeManager (GeneralsMD
/// ControlBarScheme.cpp). The field list, the two file names and the
/// selection rules were read from BFME 2's bodies and its retail field table; the scheme creation and reset rules are in BFME 2's
/// ControlBarSchemeManager::newControlBarScheme.
///
/// \see ControlBar, \ref sub_gui_apt_windows


/// \class WindowLayout
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief A group of top-level windows created together from one layout
///        file, kept as a list so they can be shown, raised and destroyed as
///        one.
///
/// A layout file (a .wnd script, or an Apt movie file) describes one screen
/// or dialog. GameWindowManager::winCreateLayout reads it through
/// WindowLayoutInfo::load: a file name whose extension is .apt (compared
/// without regard to case; the extension is the text from the name's '.',
/// found with the string class's find) goes to the Apt player, anything
/// else to the window manager's script loader. The loader returns
/// the root windows it created and the layout's callbacks and class. The
/// resulting WindowLayout then lets code treat the root windows as a unit:
/// hide or show them all, bring them all to the top of the window stack,
/// remove them, or destroy them. The shell's screen stack is made of
/// layouts (Shell::top returns one). Zero Hour: the in-game UI also keeps a
/// list of registered layouts that it updates; that list is not established
/// for BFME 2.
///
/// \par The layout block
/// A .wnd file may contain a layout block that runs from STARTLAYOUTBLOCK to
/// ENDLAYOUTBLOCK. Its entries are LAYOUTINIT, LAYOUTUPDATE, LAYOUTSHUTDOWN
/// and LAYOUTCLASS, each followed by one name. BFME 2's block parser looks
/// the init, update and shutdown names up in the function lexicon
/// (FunctionLexicon::winLayoutInitFunc and its siblings) and keeps both the
/// name and the callback; the layout's runInit, runUpdate and runShutdown
/// then call them (Zero Hour's design) with the layout and a
/// caller-supplied data pointer. The class name is looked up in a further
/// lexicon table whose purpose is not otherwise identified, and the result is
/// stored as a factory that makes the layout object. If the layout info
/// carries a factory, winCreateLayout calls it and returns null if it
/// throws; without one it makes a generic layout object and attaches the
/// info. An unknown name is not an error: the lookup yields no callback.
///
/// \par Structure and invariants
/// - The layout keeps the first window, the last window and a count. The
///   chain is linked through the windows themselves: each member window
///   stores its next and previous window in the layout and a pointer back to
///   the layout. A window therefore belongs to at most one layout.
/// - Windows are inserted at the front, so the chain runs from the most
///   recently added window to the first one added. The first window added is
///   the tail.
/// - The layout does not own its windows' memory. The window manager creates
///   and destroys them; code must remove a window from its layout before
///   the layout itself is destroyed. Zero Hour asserts that the chain is
///   empty in the destructor; destroyWindows (removing and destroying every
///   member) is the clean way to empty a layout.
/// - A hidden flag mirrors the last hide call.
///
/// \par BFME 2 differences
/// The methods are virtual (the symbol names mark addWindow, removeWindow and
/// bringForward as virtual), so a derived layout can override them. BFME 2
/// has a layout type for Apt screens, AptWindowLayout, whose base has the same
/// leading virtual slots as WindowLayout (initialise, destructor, update,
/// shutdown, hide, bring forward, add window) and which owns one game window
/// for the Apt movie; whether that base is WindowLayout itself is not
/// established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: the layout file and its layout block (callback names, class
///   name). The names must exist in the function lexicon or the layout
///   keeps the default behaviour; a layout cannot add new callbacks.
/// - If the loader adds root windows to the layout in file order (not
///   verified for BFME 2), the layout's list order, and so the order in which
///   bringForward raises them, follows the order of the window definitions in
///   the .wnd file. How the window manager itself orders windows was not
///   examined.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A layout is pure client-side window bookkeeping with no platform calls.
/// A replacement window system has to keep the add, remove and raise
/// semantics if it keeps the shell's screen stack.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The data structure and the method bodies are Zero Hour's WindowLayout,
/// carried through the BFME 1 reconstruction and checked against BFME 2's
/// bodies.
/// Virtuality comes from the BFME 2 symbol names. The layout block keywords
/// and the .apt routing are read from BFME 2's WindowLayoutInfo::load and
/// layout block parser bodies.
///
/// \see \ref sub_gui_apt_windows


/// \fn void ControlBar::addCommonCommands(Drawable *draw, bool firstDrawable)
/// \brief Merges one selected unit's command set into the multi-select bar:
///        the first unit seeds the common commands, each later unit removes
///        what it does not share.
///
/// ControlBar::populateMultiSelect calls it once for every selected
/// drawable whose object passes two tests: a template bit taken, by analogy
/// with Zero Hour, to be KINDOF_IGNORED_IN_GUI must be clear, and an object
/// status taken to be Zero Hour's OBJECT_STATUS_SOLD must be clear. It passes
/// true only for the first of them. After the last call the visible command
/// windows are the commands the whole selection has in common, plus attack
/// move (see below).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A null drawable, a drawable without an object, or an object whose
///   template carries the "ignored in the GUI" kind-of bit (the tested bit
///   is taken, by analogy with Zero Hour, to be KINDOF_IGNORED_IN_GUI) is
///   skipped without any change.
/// - The unit's command set is found by name: the object supplies a command
///   set name and the control bar looks it up in its command set table (Zero
///   Hour: ControlBar::findCommandSet on the object's command set string).
///   If the unit has no command set, nothing can be common: every common
///   command is cleared and every command window is hidden, and the function
///   returns.
/// - First unit: for each of the 32 slots, a command is common when its
///   button has the multi-select bit set in its options field. The BFME 2
///   body tests that bit by number; Zero Hour names it OK_FOR_MULTI_SELECT.
///   Whether BFME 2's CommandButton Options field gives that INI name to the
///   tested bit is not established: the name list recorded for the field
///   lists OK_FOR_MULTI_SELECT at a different position, so if that list is
///   ordered by bit, the tested bit would not carry the name (see the group
///   description, \ref grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar, for the second
///   list that does line up and why the question is unresolved). The button is stored in
///   the common-command table, its window is shown and enabled, and
///   ControlBar::setControlCommand binds the button to the window.
/// - Later units: a slot's common command survives only if this unit has the
///   very same CommandButton in that slot (compared by identity). Otherwise
///   the common command is cleared and the slot's window hidden. The one
///   exception is attack move: if either this unit's button or the stored
///   common button in the slot is the attack move command, a missing common
///   command is filled in from this unit and an existing one is kept, so
///   one unit that can attack-move gives it to the whole selection. Zero
///   Hour's source says the purpose is to let combat units be selected with
///   the odd dozer or pilot and still keep the ability.
/// - Slots are read through CommandSet::getCommandButton, so a map script's
///   run-time override of a slot (\ref page_core_frameworks_commands) takes
///   part in the merge.
///
/// \par BFME 2 differences from Zero Hour
/// - Vote step (BFME 2 only). When the first unit's common button has
///   command type number 0x24 in BFME 2's command type enumeration (what
///   that type does is not established), the button is not simply taken as
///   it is. Every selected drawable that passes the same two tests as above
///   (the template bit and the object status) has its object passed through
///   two chained lookups: an Object method that returns an object, and then a
///   method on that object that returns an interface pointer. The interface
///   is asked a boolean mode query (a virtual call whose meaning is not
///   established). A true answer adds minus one to a running total and a
///   false answer adds plus one; a unit for which either lookup yields
///   nothing abstains. The total is turned into index 0 (negative total) or
///   index 1 (zero or more) and passed to a CommandButton setter that stores
///   a selected index. The
///   setter is bounds-checked: it ignores a negative index and an index that
///   is not below the size of a list of strings held in the button, and
///   otherwise stores the index as the button's selected index. Whether that
///   stored index persists in the shared button definition, and what the
///   list holds, are not established. For every other command type the
///   first unit's object is passed to a different CommandButton method that
///   takes an object and a boolean (false here) before the button is bound;
///   what it does is not established.
/// - The attack move command is recognised by a command type number that is
///   not Zero Hour's enum value for it, so BFME 2's command type enumeration
///   is not Zero Hour's. The enumerator names are not established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: which commands survive a multi-selection is set per button in the
///   CommandButton INI block, through one bit of its options field (which
///   INI option name sets that bit in BFME 2 is not established; see above),
///   and which buttons two units share is set by their CommandSet blocks (two
///   units share a command only if their sets hold the same button in the
///   same slot).
/// - Fixed in code: 32 slots, the exception for attack move, the vote step
///   (index 0 or 1 from the sign of the total).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The function works on window objects and command definitions only and makes
/// no platform calls. The one limit a port has to carry is the fixed 32-slot
/// arrays.
///
/// \param draw          The drawable of one selected unit; null is ignored.
/// \param firstDrawable True for the first unit that populateMultiSelect
///                      accepts, which seeds the table; false for the rest.
///
/// \note Name and role come from Zero Hour's addCommonCommands, whose
/// intersection logic the body follows, and from the retail call site in
/// ControlBar::populateMultiSelect, which calls it once per selected unit. The
/// WorldBuilder build has a debug twin of the body, but that twin is unnamed
/// and was located only by a weak call-graph match, so it adds no naming
/// evidence. The vote step and the other differences above are read from the
/// BFME 2 body itself.
/// \see ControlBar::populateMultiSelect, ControlBar::setControlCommand,
///      CommandSet::getCommandButton


/// \fn void ControlBar::populateMultiSelect()
/// \brief Builds the command bar for a selection of several units: either
///        the single-unit panel of the highest-ranked unit, or the commands
///        all the units share.
///
/// ControlBar::switchToContext calls it in the multi-select arm of its
/// context switch, after hiding the panels that do not belong to that
/// context. It reads the selection from the in-game UI through a virtual
/// call at the place where Zero Hour calls InGameUI::getAllSelectedDrawables;
/// the selection is local to this client.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The function has two outcomes.
/// -# Ranked unit first (BFME 2 only). It walks the selected drawables and
///    looks for the unit with the highest experience rank. A unit counts if
///    it has an object, its template has a particular flag bit (what the
///    bit means is not established) and has clear the bit that, by analogy
///    with Zero Hour, is taken to be KINDOF_IGNORED_IN_GUI, and
///    the experience level system accepts the level handle of its
///    experience tracker. The rank is the level rank that system returns for
///    that handle. The higher rank wins; on equal ranks the unit with the
///    lower object identifier wins. If a unit qualifies, the function stores
///    its drawable as the control bar's current selected drawable, calls
///    ControlBar::populateCommand with that unit's object, and returns. The
///    bar then shows that one unit's commands, and the multi-select path is
///    not used. Zero Hour has no such pass.
/// -# Common commands. Otherwise it calls a routine that clears
///    the common-command table and, for each command window, calls another
///    helper that stores a zero in the button's data. Zero
///    Hour's equivalent, resetCommonCommandData, clears each button's
///    overlay image; the BFME 2 role of the stored value is not
///    established. It then hides all command windows and walks the
///    selection. For every drawable that has an object, has the template
///    bit above clear and whose object has the status clear that is taken,
///    by analogy with Zero Hour, to be OBJECT_STATUS_SOLD (BFME 2 evidence
///    for the exact status name is not established), it calls
///    ControlBar::addCommonCommands (true for the first, false after). It also
///    remembers the first such unit's object, and a later unit with a
///    different selected-portrait image clears the remembered image. Finally
///    it calls a portrait routine with the first such unit's object (see
///    below). Two trailing calls notify the radar window override source
///    (when one exists) and an overlay member of the control bar; their
///    roles are not established.
///
/// \par Portrait
/// The comparison of the units' selected-portrait images is computed but
/// never used: the unit passed to the portrait routine is always the first
/// accepted one, whether or not the others share its image. Zero Hour's body
/// does the same although its comment says the image is shown only when every
/// unit has it. In Zero Hour that routine is ControlBar::setPortraitByObject,
/// and BFME 2's call sits at the same place in the function. The BFME 2 call
/// lands on an empty body (retail shares one body among identical empty
/// methods), so it has no visible effect; that this call is the counterpart
/// of setPortraitByObject is inferred from the call site and is not
/// established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Which unit is picked in the first outcome depends on unit data: the
///   kind-of flags of the ThingTemplate and whether the unit has experience
///   levels (the level tables are INI data). A selection that holds one such
///   ranked unit shows that unit's own CommandSet instead of the shared
///   commands, so a mod that gives a unit these properties changes what the
///   bar shows for mixed selections.
/// - The shared commands follow the multi-select bit of each button's
///   options field and the unit CommandSets (see
///   ControlBar::addCommonCommands, which also covers why the INI option name
///   for that bit is not given here).
/// - Client-side only; the orders issued from the resulting buttons are what
///   reach the logic.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The routine reads the selection through InGameUI virtual slots and uses
/// fixed 32-entry arrays. It makes no platform calls.
///
/// \note Name and role come from Zero Hour's function of the same name. The
/// WorldBuilder build carries the same name for a debug twin, but only as a
/// low-scoring call-graph lead, so it is not the basis of the identity. The
/// identity rests on the retail evidence that this is the multi-select arm of
/// ControlBar::switchToContext, as it is in Zero Hour. The ranked-unit pass,
/// the trailing calls and the object ordering come from the BFME 2 body.
/// \see ControlBar::addCommonCommands, ControlBar::switchToContext,
///      ControlBar::populateCommand


/// \fn void ControlBarSchemeManager::init(void)
/// \brief Loads the control bar skins from the two ControlBarScheme INI
///        files.
///
/// ControlBar::init creates the manager and calls this once. It reads, in
/// this order, Data\INI\Default\ControlBarScheme.ini and
/// Data\INI\ControlBarScheme.ini. Each ControlBarScheme block in them reaches
/// INI::parseControlBarSchemeDefinition, which adds a scheme to this manager
/// (see ControlBarSchemeManager for the fields and for how a scheme is later
/// picked).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Both files are loaded with the overwrite mode (INI_LOAD_OVERWRITE) and
///   no Xfer, so a block in the second file replaces a scheme of the same
///   name from the first, and the files take no part in the INI CRC.
/// - The loader parser finds this manager through TheControlBar, so the
///   manager must already be stored in the bar when init runs; ControlBar::init
///   does that.
/// - Afterwards it tests whether any scheme was loaded. The assertion that
///   Zero Hour raises on an empty list is compiled out of the retail build,
///   so an empty list is not an error there: the function just returns, and no
///   scheme is current until one is picked.
/// - It does not pick the current scheme; ControlBar::setControlBarSchemeByPlayer
///   and the by-name and by-template variants do, later.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A mod changes or adds skins by shipping its own ControlBarScheme.ini (see
/// \ref page_modding_files for how loose files and archives compete). The
/// default file is read first, so a mod that edits the second file overrides
/// matching names without touching the default one.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The skins are bitmaps referenced by mapped image names, with positions in
/// pixels at the scheme's creation resolution. A new UI has to supply its own
/// scheme data or ignore this manager.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is Zero Hour's ControlBarSchemeManager::init as carried by the
/// BFME 1 reconstruction, with BFME 2's INI loader. The two file names are
/// string literals in the BFME 2 body, which loads no others. The
/// WorldBuilder build's assert text names this function in
/// ControlBarScheme.cpp.
/// \see ControlBarSchemeManager, ControlBar::init


/// \file GUICallbacks/LeftHUDInput.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The input callback of the radar (minimap) window.
///
/// The file holds LeftHUDInput() alone. Zero Hour keeps the same callback in
/// ControlBarCallback.cpp, beside the control bar's own callbacks.


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType LeftHUDInput(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of the radar (minimap) window: turns clicks on the
///        radar into camera moves and unit orders, and keeps the mouse
///        cursor in step with what a click would do.
///
/// The window manager calls it for mouse messages over the window that shows
/// the radar. The palantir (the Apt battle HUD that draws the radar)
/// installs it on the input window it creates (AptPalantir::init), and the
/// function lexicon lists it under the name LeftHUDInput (retail's lexicon
/// pairs that name with this function; which of the lexicon's tables holds
/// the entry was not checked, and a layout's INPUTCALLBACK entry searches
/// one table), so a .wnd layout may be able to name it. The radar object (TheRadar) does the coordinate work; this
/// function decides what a click means.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Gate. Unless the radar is forced on, a hidden radar or a local player
///   without a radar makes the callback swallow every message (it returns
///   MSG_HANDLED and does nothing), so clicks over a dead radar do not fall
///   through to the world.
/// - Hover and cursor (GWM_NONE, GWM_MOUSE_ENTERING, GWM_MOUSE_LEAVING, and
///   GWM_MOUSE_POS inside the radar). If the in-game UI has a pending GUI
///   command that needs a target position (the BFME 2 body tests one bit of
///   the command's options field; Zero Hour names that bit NEED_TARGET_POS.
///   Whether BFME 2's CommandButton Options field gives the bit that INI
///   name is not established: its recorded name list has no such entry) and
///   whose command type is one of three target-position types (Zero Hour: the
///   two special power types; BFME 2 accepts three, identified only by
///   number), the callback leaves the cursor alone on enter and leave, and
///   on mouse movement sets the cursor the command names, or the cross
///   cursor when the name is not a known cursor. Otherwise it sets the arrow
///   cursor when nothing is selected locally or the pointer is leaving, the
///   attack-move cursor when the pending command is attack move, and the
///   move cursor in every other case. The cursor is refreshed on every
///   mouse movement because hotkeys can change the pending command while
///   the pointer stays in the radar (Zero Hour's comment).
/// - Mouse position. The position word holds x in the low 16 bits and y in
///   the high 16 bits, in screen coordinates; the callback subtracts the
///   window's screen position and asks the radar to convert the pixel to
///   radar coordinates (Radar::localPixelToRadar), which fails outside the
///   map image.
/// - Clicks (GWM_LEFT_DOWN, GWM_RIGHT_DOWN). The pixel is converted to a
///   radar point and then to a world point (Radar::radarToWorld); both must
///   succeed and the radar must be visible or forced on. Then:
///   -# With no locally owned unit selected, or with the look-at button
///      (the right button, or the left when the alternate mouse option in the
///      global data is set), the tactical view looks at the point.
///   -# Else, if the pending command needs a target position, the click
///      carries it out through GameClient::evaluateContextCommand with the
///      world point and the do-command mode.
///   -# Else, if the pending command is attack move, a MSG_DO_ATTACKMOVETO
///      message with the point goes onto the message stream, the selected
///      units' voice response is played, and the pending command is cleared.
///   -# Else a MSG_DO_MOVETO message with the point is appended and the voice
///      response for the locally owned selection is played.
/// - Button releases and the middle button (GWM_LEFT_UP, GWM_RIGHT_UP,
///   GWM_MIDDLE_DOWN, GWM_MIDDLE_UP) are consumed and do nothing.
/// - Anything else returns MSG_IGNORED.
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// Zero Hour returns MSG_IGNORED at the top while the middle button is
/// down, so that middle-button handling elsewhere is not disturbed. BFME 2
/// has no such early exit and consumes middle down and up itself. BFME 2's
/// targeting test accepts three command types where Zero Hour accepts two.
/// Zero Hour's attack-move branch leaves the pending command in place, and the
/// function ends by clearing the in-game UI's attack-move mode. BFME 2 clears
/// the pending command in the attack-move branch itself and has no trailing
/// attack-move-mode call. BFME 2's voice response call also receives the
/// clicked position.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: which units, and so which commands, are affected is unit and
///   CommandButton data. If the lexicon entry is in the table that a
///   layout's INPUTCALLBACK entry searches (not checked), a layout can attach
///   this callback to any window by name, which would make that window act
///   as a radar.
/// - Orders from the radar are ordinary GameMessages (move, attack move,
///   context commands), so they take the same lockstep path as clicks in the
///   world. The cursor and camera parts are client-only.
/// - Other retail code compares a window's input callback with this
///   function's address (small getters; the code that uses them is not
///   identified); BFME 1's source does it in the in-game UI's mouseover hint
///   to let the pointer through the radar window. A replacement radar window
///   should keep this callback or keep such checks working.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The radar mapping lives in TheRadar, which the draw code also uses. A
///   different radar rendering must keep the pixel to radar and radar to
///   world conversions consistent with what it draws.
/// - Packed 16-bit mouse coordinates limit the position to 65535 pixels per
///   axis and are screen-relative; see the group notes for the 64-bit and
///   input-device issues.
///
/// \param window The radar window.
/// \param msg    The window message (GWM_*).
/// \param mData1 For mouse messages, the packed screen position.
/// \param mData2 Unused here.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED when the message was consumed (including every message
///         while the radar is unusable), otherwise MSG_IGNORED.
///
/// \note Body from Zero Hour's LeftHUDInput through the BFME 1 reconstruction,
/// adjusted to BFME 2's retail code as described above; the lexicon entry
/// for the name is retail data.
/// \see Radar::localPixelToRadar, Radar::radarToWorld, GameClient::evaluateContextCommand


/// \file GUICallbacks/MessageBox.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The system callback of the window-based message box.
///
/// Zero Hour's GUICallbacks/MessageBox.cpp also holds the MessageBox*
/// helpers that create the box. BFME 2 keeps those in another unit, and
/// they differ (see MessageBoxSystem()).


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType MessageBoxSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of the message box window: runs the callback of the
///        button that was pressed and closes the box.
///
/// A message box is a window made from the layout Menus/MessageBox.wnd (the
/// variant with a logo uses Menus/QuitMessageBox.wnd) by
/// GameWindowManager::gogoMessageBox. That function makes the window modal,
/// takes the keyboard focus for it, fills in the title and message texts,
/// stores a WindowMessageBoxData record in the window's user data, and shows
/// and raises it. The record holds up to four plain function pointers, one for
/// each of the Yes, No, Ok and Cancel buttons; a null pointer means "no
/// callback". In Zero Hour, MessageBoxSystem is the system callback for the
/// MessageBox layout, where the layout names it and the function lexicon
/// resolves the name, and a separate QuitMessageBoxSystem serves the logo
/// layout. The BFME 2 layout data is not part of this repository, so the
/// callback name each layout uses was not checked. MessageBoxSystem itself
/// matches only the four MessageBox.wnd: button names (see below), so it
/// cannot recognise the buttons of the QuitMessageBox.wnd layout. Which
/// callback BFME 2's QuitMessageBox.wnd layout uses is not established.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - GWM_DESTROY: frees the callback record and clears the window's user
///   data.
/// - GWM_INPUT_FOCUS: when the window is offered the keyboard focus (the
///   first data word is true) it writes true through the pointer in the
///   second data word, which means "I want the focus". So the box takes the
///   keyboard focus whenever it is offered.
/// - GBM_SELECTED: the first data word is the button that was pressed. Its
///   window id is compared with four name keys, for the window names
///   MessageBox.wnd:ButtonOk, MessageBox.wnd:ButtonYes,
///   MessageBox.wnd:ButtonNo and MessageBox.wnd:ButtonCancel. The keys are
///   computed once, when the first button is pressed (function-local
///   statics). The matching callback runs if it is not null, and then the
///   message box window is destroyed through the window manager. A press on
///   any other control does nothing and leaves the box open.
/// - Every other message returns MSG_IGNORED. BFME 2 handles GWM_CREATE by
///   the default (ignored) path.
///
/// \par BFME 2 and the creation path
/// BFME 2's MessageBoxOk, MessageBoxYesNo and MessageBoxOkCancel wrappers
/// wrap their callbacks in a reference-counted holder and pass them, with
/// the title and body text, to a shared prompt-creation routine, instead of
/// calling gogoMessageBox as Zero Hour's versions do. So those wrappers are
/// not how a window of this kind is made in BFME 2, and which callers reach
/// gogoMessageBox is not established: it is a virtual method, so it is
/// normally reached through the vtable and direct call sites are not the way
/// to find its users. BFME 1's function lexicon registers this
/// callback by name; the BFME 2 lexicon entry was not checked. What is
/// established is that gogoMessageBox builds the record this callback reads,
/// with Zero Hour's four callbacks and button names.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: the box's look and button positions come from the
///   Menus/MessageBox.wnd layout (or Menus/QuitMessageBox.wnd for the logo
///   variant) in the archives. The window names that gogoMessageBox looks up
///   (MessageBoxParent, StaticTextTitle, StaticTextMessage and the four
///   buttons ButtonOk, ButtonYes, ButtonNo and ButtonCancel) are fixed in
///   code. The body reads the positions of ButtonOk, ButtonNo and
///   ButtonCancel without a null test, and moves and shows ButtonYes (again
///   without a null test) when the Yes flag is passed, so a replacement
///   layout should keep these names. gogoMessageBox prefixes
///   them with MessageBox.wnd: for Menus/MessageBox.wnd and with
///   QuitMessageBox.wnd: for Menus/QuitMessageBox.wnd. This callback only
///   matches the four MessageBox.wnd: button names.
/// - The title and body strings come from the caller.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The callbacks have no arguments and no user data, so a callback cannot
///   tell which box called it. The first data word of GBM_SELECTED carries a
///   window pointer through a 32-bit word.
///
/// \param window The message box window.
/// \param msg    The window message.
/// \param mData1 GWM_INPUT_FOCUS: whether focus is being offered. GBM_SELECTED:
///               the pressed button window.
/// \param mData2 GWM_INPUT_FOCUS: a pointer to a bool that receives true.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for the three messages above, MSG_IGNORED otherwise.
///
/// \warning GBM_SELECTED reads the callback record without a null test, so a
/// box that has no record in its user data crashes on the first button press.
/// The creator must install the record before the box can be used.
/// \see GadgetPushButtonInput


/// \file Gadget/GadgetPushButtonInput.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The input callback of push buttons, check-like buttons included.
///
/// The button's system callback and its accessors live in other files (see
/// \ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets). The file holds GadgetPushButtonInput() alone.


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetPushButtonInput(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of push buttons: press and release handling,
///        check-like toggling, right clicks, auto-repeat, keyboard
///        activation and the click sound.
///
/// In BFME 2 it is reached through the input method of the standard push
/// button window subclasses: both the plain one and the image-drawn one call
/// it directly. The scroll bar buttons of list boxes
/// (GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar()) are created with the image status bit,
/// which makes the device-level push button factory choose the image-drawn
/// subclass, so they take this path. BFME 2 also has a separate button window
/// class whose input method goes through a wrapper for palantir command
/// buttons: the wrapper plays its own sound events
/// (Gui_PalantirCommandButtonClick and
/// Gui_PalantirCommandButtonDisabledClick) first and only then calls
/// GadgetPushButtonInput(). Both paths therefore end in this function. Which
/// of these window classes the control bar's command windows use is not
/// established: ControlBar::setControlCommand only tests whether the window
/// is a push button, and the layout data that would choose the class is not
/// among the sources this page was derived from. A button never acts on
/// the game itself: it changes its own state and tells its owner window by
/// sending GBM_SELECTED (or GBM_SELECTED_RIGHT) to it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// BFME 2's push button user data holds the button's own sound reference and
/// a repeat delay in milliseconds (Zero Hour's PushButtonData has the
/// alternate sound but no repeat delay, and Zero Hour's push button has no
/// auto-repeat). The window status holds the CHECK_LIKE, RIGHT_CLICK and
/// on-mouse-down bits. A small helper decides whether the button "triggers on
/// mouse down": true when the on-mouse-down status bit is set or the button
/// is check-like (Zero Hour calls it buttonTriggersOnMouseDown).
/// - GWM_LEFT_DOWN. Raises the click sound (below). A check-like button
///   toggles its selected state, any other button becomes selected. If the
///   button triggers on mouse down, it sends GBM_SELECTED to the owner at
///   once, and stores a shared repeat timestamp of the current time plus
///   twice the repeat delay.
/// - GWM_LEFT_UP. Only a selected, non-check-like button reacts. If it did
///   not trigger on mouse down it sends GBM_SELECTED now; then it clears the
///   selected state. Any other release returns MSG_IGNORED, because it was
///   not meant for this button.
/// - GWM_MOUSE_LEAVING. Clears the highlight (when the style tracks the
///   mouse) and tells the owner (GBM_MOUSE_LEAVING). A non-check-like button
///   also drops its selected state, so sliding off a pressed button cancels
///   the click. If the parent is a horizontal slider the parent's highlight
///   is cleared too.
/// - GWM_MOUSE_ENTERING sets the highlight when the style tracks the mouse,
///   tells the owner (GBM_MOUSE_ENTERING) and highlights a horizontal-slider
///   parent; unlike leaving, it does not change the selected state.
/// - GWM_LEFT_DRAG forwards GGM_LEFT_DRAG to the owner.
/// - GWM_RIGHT_DOWN and GWM_RIGHT_UP work like the left ones, but only for
///   buttons that carry the right-click status bit and send
///   GBM_SELECTED_RIGHT. Without that bit they return MSG_IGNORED.
/// - GWM_CHAR. Enter and Space act like a click: on key-down a check-like
///   button toggles and notifies, any other button becomes selected; on
///   key-up a selected non-check-like button notifies and deselects. Tab
///   moves the focus on key-down: to the previous control when the left Shift
///   modifier is held, otherwise to the next. Every other key returns
///   MSG_IGNORED (Zero Hour also moved focus with the arrow keys).
/// - Auto-repeat (message number 24, which is Zero Hour's mouse-position
///   message GWM_MOUSE_POS). While the button is selected and
///   triggers on mouse down, and its repeat delay is not zero, this message
///   sends another GBM_SELECTED with a null second word whenever more than
///   the repeat delay has passed since the shared timestamp, and then resets
///   the timestamp. Because the press set the timestamp two delays into the
///   future, the first repeat comes after about three delays and later ones
///   every delay. The message always returns MSG_IGNORED so other windows
///   still see it. How often the window manager sends this message to a
///   pressed button is not established.
/// - The timestamp is one static value shared by all push buttons, read
///   against timeGetTime. Because the comparison is unsigned, a clock wrap
///   (about 49 days of uptime) while a button is held can stop its repeat
///   until the next press.
///
/// \par The click sound
/// A left press, and a right press on a right-click button, builds an audio
/// event, sets a small integer attribute on it, copies the button's own sound
/// reference into it when the button has one, and gives it to TheAudio when
/// an audio manager exists. The sound reference is stored in the button's
/// data by a small gadget helper. The helper
/// takes an audio event name and passes it to an audio manager method that
/// turns an event name into a reference handle; the helper stores that
/// handle in the button's data. (Zero Hour stores
/// the name itself and looks it up when the click is raised.)
///
/// The names come from code, not from layout or INI data.
/// ControlBar::setControlCommand attaches the audio event GUIControlButtonClick
/// to each window it binds a command button to (it returns early for a window
/// that is not a push button), and ControlBar::populateSpecialPowerShortcut
/// then gives the special power shortcut buttons GUIGenShortcutClick instead.
/// Both names are string literals in those bodies.
/// Zero Hour falls back to the audio event GUIClick when a button has no
/// alternate sound.
/// This BFME 2 body sets no fallback name: a button nobody gave a sound to
/// raises an event whose reference is empty, and what the audio manager does
/// with that is not established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Fixed in code, not data: which audio event a button raises. The event
///   names above are literals in the control bar code, a .wnd file has no
///   push button data block that could carry a sound, and the CommandButton
///   INI block has no click sound key (see the group notes). A mod can
///   redefine what GUIControlButtonClick and GUIGenShortcutClick play, through
///   the audio event definitions (\ref sub_audio_modding), but cannot give
///   one button a different event without a code change. The two palantir
///   command button events named above are string literals in the wrapper's
///   code as well.
/// - Fixed in code: the repeat delay. No .wnd key sets it. The one setter call
///   reviewed is in GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar(), which gives the list box
///   arrow buttons 150 ms.
/// - Data: the on-mouse-down, right-click and check-like bits are window
///   status flags, and the script parser's status-name list holds ON_MOUSE_DOWN,
///   RIGHT_CLICK and CHECK_LIKE, so a layout can set them in a STATUS entry
///   (\ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers). For command buttons,
///   ControlBar::setControlCommand switches the window's check-like mode on or
///   off from one bit of the CommandButton options field. That bit's INI name
///   is not established for BFME 2: Zero Hour calls it CHECK_LIKE, but BFME
///   2's recorded name list for the CommandButton Options field differs from
///   Zero Hour's, so do not rely on that name until it is checked
///   (\ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets).
/// - Client-side only: a button press sends window messages, never game
///   messages.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Auto-repeat and the shared timestamp depend on timeGetTime and on the
///   frequency of the repeat-driving message. A port should supply a
///   monotonic clock and keep the three-delay first-repeat rule.
/// - The click sound goes through TheAudio->addAudioEvent, a virtual slot of
///   the audio manager, so any audio back end that keeps that interface
///   works.
/// - Tab order is the window manager's (winNextTab and winPrevTab); other
///   navigation (arrow keys, game pad) is not handled here.
///
/// \param window The push button.
/// \param msg    The window message (GWM_*).
/// \param mData1 Message dependent: the packed mouse position for mouse
///               messages (passed on as the second word of GBM_SELECTED), the
///               key code for GWM_CHAR.
/// \param mData2 For GWM_CHAR, the key state bits (up, down).
/// \return MSG_HANDLED when the message was used, MSG_IGNORED for the repeat
///         message, for releases not meant for this button and for unhandled
///         keys.
///
/// \note The body follows Zero Hour's GadgetPushButton.cpp plus the auto-repeat
/// and Shift-Tab handling that BFME 1 already has. The differences listed above
/// come from comparing both bodies.
/// \see GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar, GadgetListBoxInput


/// \file Gadget/GadgetComboBoxSystem.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The system callback of combo boxes.
///
/// The combo box's input callback, its accessors and its colour and font
/// setters live in other files (\ref grp_w1_gui_gadgets,
/// \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api and \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists).
/// This file holds GadgetComboBoxSystem() alone.


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetComboBoxSystem(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief System callback of combo boxes: handles the messages that screens
///        send to a combo box, and the notifications from its three child
///        windows.
///
/// A combo box is a window with three children: a drop-down button, an edit
/// box (a text entry field) and a list box that stays hidden until the
/// button is pressed. This callback is the glue. It forwards the combo box's
/// public messages to the right child, copies a chosen row's text into the
/// edit box, keeps the list box sized and positioned, and reports changes to
/// the combo box's owner window with GCM_SELECTED. The wrappers that screens
/// really call (GadgetComboBoxAddEntry and its siblings, \ref
/// grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api) just send these messages. In BFME 2
/// the callback is reached from the system method of the combo box window
/// subclass.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Message names are Zero Hour's; BFME 2 numbers some of them differently.
/// Messages from screens (the first data word is the first argument):
/// - GGM_SET_LABEL: sets the window's own text from the UnicodeString the
///   first word points at.
/// - GCM_GET_TEXT and GCM_SET_TEXT: read the edit box text into the
///   UnicodeString the second word points at, or write the string the first
///   word points at into it. Without an edit box they do nothing.
/// - GCM_ADD_ENTRY: first word is a pointer to the text, second the text
///   colour. Counts the entry; when the count reaches the list box's
///   capacity the capacity is doubled; appends the text as a new row of
///   column 0 (row -1 means "append", with the overwrite flag set); and
///   returns the list box's add result, the new row index, through the
///   return value (cast to WindowMsgHandledType). It then resizes the
///   list: it shows as many rows as there are entries up to the combo box's
///   maximum display count, each row being one font height plus 2 pixels,
///   plus 4 pixels, positioned directly under the edit box. When every row
///   fits, the list box's up and down buttons and slider are hidden and the
///   list is 16 pixels wider than the combo box; with more rows than fit they
///   are shown and the list has the combo box's width. If the list box
///   window is enabled, the drop-down button is enabled exactly when the
///   combo box holds more than one entry (BFME 2 only).
/// - A BFME 2 combo box message with no Zero Hour counterpart (message value
///   0x4023, between the add-entry and delete-entry messages; it replaces the
///   text of one row): first word is a
///   pointer to a new text, second the row. If the row's current text differs, it is replaced with
///   the same colour; and if that row is the selected row, or nothing is
///   selected, the edit box text is updated to the new text too. Always
///   returns MSG_HANDLED.
/// - GCM_DEL_ALL: empties the list box, clears the edit text, sets the entry
///   count to zero and disables the drop-down button (the last step is BFME 2
///   only). GCM_DEL_ENTRY is accepted and does nothing in both games.
/// - GCM_SET_SELECTION: first word is the row, second word true means "do
///   not hide the list for the selection notification that follows" (it
///   only has an effect if the list is currently shown). The row is selected
///   in the list box. GCM_GET_SELECTION stores the selected row, or -1
///   without a list box, through the Int the second word points at.
/// - GCM_SET_ITEM_DATA and GCM_GET_ITEM_DATA: write or read the pointer-sized
///   item data of column 0 of a row (row in the first word; the data in or
///   through the second word).
/// - GGM_CLOSE: hides the drop-down list (Zero Hour: HideListBox).
/// - GGM_RESIZED: width and height arrive in the two words. Only when the
///   list is hidden it resizes the (hidden) list box to the new size,
///   positions the edit box at the origin with the width left over by the
///   button, and puts the drop-down button at the right edge. BFME 2 sizes
///   the button from its enabled image: height equal to the combo box's
///   height and width scaled by the image's aspect ratio. Zero Hour keeps the
///   button's own size.
/// - GWM_INPUT_FOCUS: sets or clears the highlight, tells the owner with
///   GGM_FOCUS_CHANGE (first word the gained flag, second the combo box's
///   window id), passes the focus message on to the edit box, which is the
///   child that really takes the keyboard, and answers true: the combo box
///   always accepts focus.
/// - GWM_DESTROY: clears the window manager's lone window (the single popup
///   that closes when a click lands elsewhere; this closes any open combo
///   box when screens change) if the window manager still exists, and frees
///   the combo box data.
/// - A window message numbered 28, which Zero Hour does not have (its
///   purpose beyond the effect described here is not established): disables
///   the drop-down button when the combo box holds one entry or none.
/// - GWM_CREATE and GGM_LEFT_DRAG are accepted and ignored.
///
/// Notifications from the children:
/// - GBM_SELECTED from the drop-down button: runs the drop-down toggle
///   (OnComboBoxClick), which shows or hides the list box and makes the combo
///   box the window manager's lone window while it is shown. BFME 2's toggle
///   then raises a click sound whatever the list's audio feedback flag says;
///   the sound reference comes from the same misc-audio entry as the list box
///   click (see the modding notes of GadgetListBoxInput()).
/// - GLM_SELECTED from the list box: hides the list unless the "do not hide"
///   flag was set (which it then clears); when a row was chosen (not -1) it
///   copies that row's column 0 text and colour into the edit box and sends
///   GCM_SELECTED (second word 0) to the owner.
/// - GEM_UPDATE_TEXT from the edit box (it reports a text update): sends
///   GCM_UPDATE_TEXT to the owner, clears the list box selection and hides
///   the list.
/// - GEM_EDIT_DONE from the edit box (it reports that editing is done): hides the list, sends
///   GCM_SELECTED to the owner with -1 in the second word (meaning "typed
///   text, no list row"; Zero Hour sends 0) and moves the focus to the next
///   control (BFME 2 only).
/// - GGM_FOCUS_CHANGE reporting the edit box's id: sets the edit box's
///   draw-text-from-start flag, cleared when the focus is gained and set
///   when it is lost (BFME 2 only).
/// Anything else returns MSG_IGNORED.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: BFME 2's parseComboBoxData reads five values from the COMBOBOXDATA
///   block of a .wnd layout: the editable flag, the maximum character count,
///   the maximum number of rows shown, and two character filters (ASCII only,
///   and letters and numbers only). Like LISTBOXDATA, the values are read by
///   position and their labels are skipped (see
///   \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers). The rows are added by the
///   screen's code.
/// - Not in the layout: the list box inside the combo box. For a combo box
///   made from a .wnd file, the script loader fills that list's creation data
///   in code (a list length of 10, a scroll bar, force-select, one column, no
///   multi-select), and GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox switches the
///   list's audio feedback on. The layout has no block for that inner list.
/// - Fixed in code: the row height formula, the 16 pixel widening, the
///   doubling of the list capacity and the way a typed text is reported (-1).
///   A screen that reads GCM_SELECTED must treat -1 as "typed text".
/// - Client-side only.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Heights use the font height from the window manager and fixed pixel
///   margins (2, 4 and 16), which do not scale with the resolution.
/// - Several messages pass pointers to stack objects through 32-bit data
///   words (strings, an Int, a bool); see the group notes.
/// - The lone window mechanism and keyboard focus belong to the window
///   manager; a replacement window system needs equivalents so the drop-down
///   list closes on an outside click.
///
/// \param window The combo box.
/// \param msg    The message (GGM_*, GCM_*, GBM_*, GLM_*, GEM_*, GWM_*).
/// \param mData1 First data word; meaning per message above.
/// \param mData2 Second data word; meaning per message above.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED for handled messages, MSG_IGNORED otherwise. For
///         GCM_ADD_ENTRY the return value is the new row index instead (cast
///         to the handled type).
///
/// \note Body from Zero Hour's GadgetComboBox.cpp through the BFME 1
/// reconstruction; the differences marked above are what BFME 2's own body
/// adds. The children are fetched through small accessors whose Zero Hour
/// names are swapped relative to BFME 2's layout (see
/// \ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists).
/// \see GadgetComboBoxAddEntry, GadgetListBoxSetSelected


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListBoxMultiInput.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The input callbacks of list boxes, their hit test and scroll
///        helpers.
///
/// Despite its name the file holds both list box input callbacks:
/// GadgetListBoxMultiInput() for multi-select lists and GadgetListBoxInput()
/// for ordinary single-select lists. It also holds the hit test
/// getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord() and its public wrapper
/// GadgetListBoxGetEntryBasedOnXY(), a helper that finds the last visible
/// row, the scroll-to-row helper used by the type-ahead search and
/// GadgetListBoxSetTopVisibleEntry(). The list box system callback, the
/// add and reset functions and the model of the list data are in other
/// files (\ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists, \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api).
///
/// \par The list data these callbacks use
/// Rows are an array of records, each with the row's height, its bottom edge
/// (the running total of heights, in list pixels) and its cells. The list has a
/// count of rows in use, a display height and a display position (the pixel
/// offset of the top of the visible area). A single-select list keeps one
/// selected row index, -1 for none. A multi-select list keeps an array of
/// selected row indexes that ends with -1. BFME 2's list data has several
/// per-list flags that Zero Hour's lacks and that these callbacks read: one
/// that stops all selection, one that makes the list scroll freely by
/// pixels instead of selecting rows, one that stops the total height from
/// growing, and one that makes the list report which row the pointer is
/// over; plus a value that records which column the last mouse click landed
/// in. Where those flags are set from is not established.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: the MULTISELECT entry of the LISTBOXDATA block of a .wnd layout
///   makes a list multi-select (the other keys of that block are listed under
///   GadgetListBoxInput(); see also \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers). In
///   BFME 2 the flag takes effect through GadgetListBoxSetListLength:
///   GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetListBox passes the list length to it, and
///   when the list data's multi-select flag is set it calls a helper that
///   allocates the selection array, fills it with -1
///   and installs GadgetListBoxMultiInput() as the window's input function.
///   In Zero Hour gogoGadgetListBox installs the callback itself.
/// - Not only data: screen code also calls that helper directly (some Apt
///   screens' gadget set-up does), so a list can be made multi-select without
///   the layout asking for it.
/// - The key bindings and the double-click rule are fixed in code.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// See GadgetListBoxInput() for the double-click timer and clock issues.


/// \fn static Int getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord(GameWindow *window, Int x, Int y, Int &row, Int &column)
/// \brief Hit test: finds the row and column of a list box under a screen
///        point.
///
/// The public wrapper GadgetListBoxGetEntryBasedOnXY() forwards to it, and
/// both list box input callbacks call it only to follow the pointer when a
/// list tracks which row is under the mouse; their click and right-click
/// handling repeats the same walk inline.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The list's origin is the window's screen position. When the window has
///   a title, the title takes one font height plus one pixel off the top
///   (font height from the window manager).
/// - Rows are walked from the first: each row stores its bottom edge in list
///   pixels, so the row under the pointer is the first one whose bottom edge
///   is greater than the pointer's vertical offset from the origin plus the
///   display position.
/// - The walk stops with row -1 (no row) when it reaches the number of rows
///   in use, which is the empty space below the last row, or when the
///   previous row's bottom edge is already beyond the visible area.
/// - With a row found, the column is the first one whose running width
///   exceeds the pointer's horizontal offset from the window's left edge.
///   A pointer beyond the last column gives column -1. A pointer to the left
///   of the window is not tested and lands in the first column.
/// - Both results are written through the reference parameters, and the row
///   is also the return value.
///
/// \param window The list box.
/// \param x      Screen x of the point.
/// \param y      Screen y of the point.
/// \param row    [out] The row index, or -1 when the point is not over a row.
/// \param column [out] The column index, or -1 when no row or no column was
///                     hit.
/// \return The row index (same as row).
///
/// \note Zero Hour's static function of the same name; retail keeps one
/// copy that the wrapper tail-calls.
/// \see GadgetListBoxInput, GadgetListBoxMultiInput


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetListBoxMultiInput(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of multi-select list boxes: toggles the clicked row
///        in the selection, reports clicks and scrolls with the wheel.
///
/// In BFME 2 it is called from the input method of the multi-select list box
/// window class, and a list-box helper that turns multi-selection on also
/// installs it as the window's input callback. Zero
/// Hour: GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetListBox installs it for list boxes
/// whose data asks for multiple selection. It is a smaller callback than
/// GadgetListBoxInput(): the keyboard is not used for selection.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - GWM_LEFT_UP. If the list has the no-select flag, nothing happens. The
///   list takes the keyboard focus, the row under the pointer is found by
///   the same walk as getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord() (repeated inline here), and a disabled row, empty space or a
///   point outside the visible rows counts as -1. The row is then toggled in
///   the selection array: if it is already in the array it is removed (the
///   rest of the array shifts up), otherwise it is appended and the array is
///   terminated with -1. The remembered click column is reset to -1. The
///   list's owner receives GLM_SELECTED with the clicked row, which is -1
///   for empty space; the owner reads the whole selection with
///   GadgetListBoxGetSelected.
/// - GWM_RIGHT_UP. Takes the focus and sends GLM_RIGHT_CLICKED to the owner.
///   The second word points at a record of the pointer's x, y and the row (-1
///   for none); the record lives on this function's stack and is valid only
///   during the send. Disabled rows are not special-cased.
/// - GWM_WHEEL_UP and GWM_WHEEL_DOWN. They work like pressing the scroll
///   bar's buttons and so only do anything if the list has such a button
///   (a scroll bar): down scrolls one step when the visible bottom is not
///   past the total height, up when the display position is above zero.
/// - GWM_MOUSE_ENTERING and GWM_MOUSE_LEAVING. For a list whose style tracks
///   the mouse, set or clear the highlight and notify the owner
///   (GBM_MOUSE_ENTERING or GBM_MOUSE_LEAVING).
/// - GWM_LEFT_DRAG. Forwards GGM_LEFT_DRAG to the owner for a mouse-tracking
///   list.
/// - Message 24 (mouse position in Zero Hour). When the list tracks the
///   row under the pointer, it stores that row in the list data (the
///   hit-test result) and forwards the message to the owner with the
///   position word. BFME 2 only.
/// - GWM_LEFT_DOWN. Plays the list box click sound if the list has audio
///   feedback enabled, and returns MSG_HANDLED so the matching release
///   reaches the list.
/// - GWM_CHAR. Only Tab on key-down moves the focus to the next control; any
///   other key returns MSG_IGNORED.
/// - Any other message returns MSG_IGNORED.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A screen that uses a multi-select list reads the selection with
/// GadgetListBoxGetSelected and reacts to GLM_SELECTED; there is no keyboard
/// selection to account for.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The selection array is sized by the list length and ends with -1; the
/// toggle writes a terminator one entry after the last selected row, which
/// has no explicit bounds check against the capacity.
///
/// \param window The list box.
/// \param msg    The window message.
/// \param mData1 For mouse messages, the packed screen position.
/// \param mData2 Message dependent; unused for most messages here.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED when the message was used, MSG_IGNORED otherwise.
///
/// \note The no-select flag, the disabled-row rule and the position message
/// are BFME 2 additions to Zero Hour's body.
/// \see GadgetListBoxInput, getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord


/// \fn WindowMsgHandledType GadgetListBoxInput(GameWindow *window, UnsignedInt msg, WindowMsgData mData1, WindowMsgData mData2)
/// \brief Input callback of single-select list boxes: mouse selection with
///        double-click detection, keyboard navigation, type-ahead search and
///        mouse wheel scrolling.
///
/// In BFME 2 it is the default input behaviour of the ordinary list box
/// window class: that class's input method calls the input callback stored
/// on the window when there is one, and this function otherwise. Zero Hour:
/// GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetListBox installs it on every list box that
/// is not multi-select. The list reports to its owner window with
/// GLM_SELECTED (a row was chosen or cleared), GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED and
/// GLM_RIGHT_CLICKED; the second data word carries the row (or a pointer to a
/// click record for right clicks). In BFME 2, code that reacts to these
/// notifications includes the Apt score screen's system handler
/// (AptScoreScreen::OnSystemMsg, GLM_SELECTED), the Apt save/load screen's
/// list event handler (GLM_SELECTED and GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED on its game and
/// autosave lists) and AptOnlineCustomMatch::GameWindowSystem
/// (GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED from one of its lists); this is not a complete list.
///
/// \par Mouse handling
/// Every click sound below goes through one small routine that does nothing
/// unless the list's audio feedback flag is on (set by code, not by the
/// layout; see the modding notes below).
/// - GWM_LEFT_DOWN plays the list click sound if the list has audio feedback
///   on, and is consumed so that the release arrives here.
/// - GWM_LEFT_UP does the selecting. With the no-select flag nothing happens.
///   The list takes the keyboard focus. The row under the pointer is found
///   by the same walk as getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord() (repeated inline);
///   empty space and rows beyond the
///   visible area give -1, and so does a disabled row. Clicking the row that
///   is already selected deselects it (-1) unless the list is force-select.
///   In a force-select list a click that would clear the selection (on empty
///   space or on a disabled row) keeps the old selection and does not send
///   GLM_SELECTED (Zero Hour sends GLM_SELECTED with the old row). That only
///   suppresses GLM_SELECTED: the double-click check described below is made
///   first and independently, so GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED can still be sent for
///   such a click. The column under the pointer is worked out from the
///   column widths and remembered in the list data (-1 when the click is not
///   over a column). If the list's own previous click time plus the system
///   double-click interval is still in the future, and the click is on the
///   previously selected row (or on a valid row when nothing was selected),
///   GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED is sent with that row. The code sets the stored click
///   time to zero there, but it is set to the current time again right after
///   the check, so a third quick click on the row is another double click
///   (Zero Hour's body does the same). GLM_SELECTED is then sent with the
///   new selection, except in the force-select case above.
/// - GWM_RIGHT_DOWN plays the click sound; GWM_RIGHT_UP takes the focus and
///   sends GLM_RIGHT_CLICKED with a pointer to a record (pointer x and y, and
///   the row under it or -1) that lives on this function's stack.
/// - GWM_MOUSE_POS. While another window is the window manager's lone window
///   (the open drop-down of a combo box, for example), a list box that is
///   neither that window nor related to it by a window-relationship test
///   (probably a parent/child test; not established) ignores the message
///   (MSG_IGNORED); so
///   lists under an open combo box list probably do not highlight. Otherwise a list
///   that tracks the row under the pointer stores that row in its data and
///   forwards the message to the owner.
/// - Mouse enter, leave and drag only matter for lists whose style tracks
///   the mouse; they set or clear the highlight and notify the owner.
/// - GWM_WHEEL_UP and GWM_WHEEL_DOWN scroll by one step, or by one line (the
///   font height, 20 pixels without a font) in a free-scroll list. Unlike the
///   multi-select list, no scroll bar button is needed.
///
/// \par Keyboard handling (key-down unless noted)
/// - Enter and Space, on key-up and without a Control-type modifier: play
///   the click sound and send GLM_DOUBLE_CLICKED with the selected row (which
///   may be -1). With the modifier the key is ignored.
/// - Up, Down, Page Up, Page Down, Home and End: in a free-scroll list they
///   scroll the view by a line, a page, to the top or to the bottom and do
///   not change the selection. In an ordinary list they move the selection
///   (the first press with nothing selected selects the first row, Page Up
///   and Page Down move by the number of rows between the first and last
///   visible ones), scroll just enough to bring the row fully into view, clear
///   the remembered click column and send GLM_SELECTED. End and Home do not
///   test for an empty list.
/// - Tab moves the focus to the next control, or the previous one with the
///   left Shift modifier held.
/// - Any other key does a type-ahead search (ordinary lists only): starting
///   after the current selection and wrapping around, it looks for a row
///   whose first text cell begins with the typed character, trying each
///   shift state of the key the keyboard can report. On a hit it selects
///   and centres the row, clears the click column, sends GLM_SELECTED and
///   plays the click sound. With no hit, or in a free-scroll list, the key is
///   ignored (MSG_IGNORED).
///
/// \par Differences from Zero Hour
/// BFME 2 adds Page Up, Page Down, Home and End, free-scroll lists, the
/// no-select flag, disabled rows, the remembered click column, the mouse
/// row tracking message with its lone window test, the Control test on Enter
/// and Space, and Shift-Tab. It drops tabbing with the arrow keys (Zero Hour
/// moved focus with Left and Right). The force-select rule above (no
/// GLM_SELECTED when the old selection is kept) also differs.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data, from a layout: a .wnd file gives a list box its creation settings in
///   a LISTBOXDATA block. BFME 2's parseListboxData reads LENGTH, AUTOSCROLL,
///   an optional SCROLLIFATEND, AUTOPURGE, SCROLLBAR, MULTISELECT, COLUMNS
///   (followed by one width for each column when there is more than one) and
///   FORCESELECT, and nothing else. The parser reads the values by position,
///   not by name: apart from the check for the optional SCROLLIFATEND label,
///   it does not compare the label words, so the entries must keep this
///   order. The ones that matter to the input code here are MULTISELECT (the
///   list takes the multi-select path, see GadgetListBoxMultiInput()),
///   FORCESELECT (the force-select rule above) and SCROLLBAR (whether
///   GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar() runs).
/// - Not in the layout: the audio feedback flag, the one that gates the click
///   sound. LISTBOXDATA has no key for it. It is set only through the code
///   API (GadgetListBoxSetAudioFeedback(), see
///   \ref grp_w1018_gui_listbox_combobox_api), so it is the screen's or the
///   window factory's choice, and a layout cannot turn it on or off. For
///   example, GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetComboBox switches it on for the
///   list box inside every combo box. The flags BFME 2 added to the list data
///   (no-select, free-scroll, fixed total height and row tracking) are not
///   LISTBOXDATA keys either, and where they are set was not traced.
/// - The click sound very probably comes from the MiscAudio INI block (the
///   ComboBoxClick entry) rather than from the list; this is inferred from a
///   matching table offset, not from a traced assignment. When audio feedback
///   is on, the BFME 2 click routine builds
///   its audio event from a sound reference held in the audio manager's
///   misc-audio block; Zero Hour names the event GUIComboBoxClick in code
///   instead. The MiscAudio field table in BFME 2 has a ComboBoxClick entry
///   (Zero Hour has none) at the offset that routine reads, and the combo
///   box drop-down toggle reads the same offset. The inference relies on
///   that matching offset (the GameSpy chat code, for one, reads the entry at
///   the offset that the same table gives PrivateMessageNotification), so
///   check it before relying on it. A mod that wants a different list click would
///   edit that entry or the audio event it names (\ref sub_audio_modding).
/// - Fixed in code: the key bindings, the 20 pixel line for a list without a
///   font, and which messages are sent. A screen must treat -1 in
///   GLM_SELECTED as "nothing selected".
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The double-click rule reads timeGetTime and a global interval that a
///   start-up initializer fills from GetDoubleClickTime, plus one timestamp
///   per list. A port needs both.
/// - Keyboard handling uses the keyboard object's modifier flags and its
///   printable-key lookup for type-ahead, so it assumes the Windows key
///   code table. International keyboards work only through that lookup.
/// - The pointer is packed in 16 bits per axis; see the group notes.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body is Zero Hour's GadgetListBoxInput through the BFME 1
/// reconstruction. The only WorldBuilder counterpart found is an unnamed,
/// low-score call-graph lead, which carries no naming or behaviour evidence.
/// The behaviour described here is from the BFME 2 body.
///
/// \param window The list box.
/// \param msg    The window message.
/// \param mData1 For mouse messages, the packed screen position; for
///               GWM_CHAR, the key code.
/// \param mData2 For GWM_CHAR, the key state bits.
/// \return MSG_HANDLED when the message was used, MSG_IGNORED otherwise (also
///         for unmatched keys and, with a lone window, mouse positions).
///
/// \warning The End and Home keys read a row before checking that the list
/// has one, so on an empty ordinary list the End key indexes the row before
/// the first.
/// \see GadgetListBoxMultiInput, getListboxEntryBasedOnCoord


/// \file Gadget/GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1023b_gui_gadget_input_controlbar
/// \brief The factory for a list box's scroll bar children.
///
/// The file holds GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar() alone.


/// \fn void GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar(GameWindow *listbox)
/// \brief Creates the scroll bar of a list box: an up button, a down button
///        and a vertical slider, all children of the list.
///
/// GameWindowManager::gogoGadgetListBox calls it, as its only caller, when
/// the list data asks for a scroll bar (the SCROLLBAR entry of the LISTBOXDATA
/// block, see \ref grp_w1012_gui_wnd_script_parsers). The created windows are
/// remembered in the list data (up button, down button and slider) and the
/// list system callback (\ref grp_w3_gui_gadget_lists) keeps the slider's
/// range and position in step with the list's scrolling.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The bar sits against the list's right edge, below the title when there
///   is one. A title takes one font height plus one pixel, as everywhere
///   else in the list code.
/// - Each button is 21 by 22 pixels at a 1024 by 768 design, scaled by the
///   current resolution in the global data (width times the horizontal
///   resolution over 1024, height times the vertical resolution over 768,
///   integer arithmetic). The slider has the same width. The margins are
///   fixed pixel constants that do not scale: the buttons and the slider sit
///   2 pixels in from the list's right edge; the up button sits 2 pixels
///   below the title offset; the slider starts 3 pixels below the title offset
///   plus one button height; the down button ends 2 pixels above the bottom;
///   and the slider length is the list height (less the title offset, when
///   there is a title) less two button heights and 6 pixels.
/// - The children copy the list's status bits minus border, hidden and
///   no-input, are flagged as image windows, and are made active and enabled.
///   Their style is push button (twice) and vertical slider, with the
///   mouse-track style copied from the list. The list itself is each child's
///   owner, so the buttons' GBM_SELECTED and the slider's notifications arrive
///   at the list's system callback.
/// - The buttons come from the window manager's push button factory and the
///   slider from its slider factory, with an all-zero slider range (the list's
///   own code sets the real range as rows come and go, as in Zero Hour). No
///   fonts are given.
/// - Both arrow buttons then get a repeat delay of 150 (milliseconds) and the
///   on-mouse-down status bit through a gadget helper that also creates
///   missing push button data. With that, GadgetPushButtonInput() sends the
///   button's GBM_SELECTED when it is pressed and repeats it while it is
///   held, which scrolls the list.
/// - Finally the list data's scroll bar flag is set.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data: whether a list has a scroll bar. The images and colours of the
///   buttons and slider are not set here: the factory calls ask the window
///   manager for its default gadget look, so they come from there.
/// - Fixed in code: the 21 by 22 size, the 150 ms repeat delay and the margins.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Button sizes follow the resolution against a 1024 by 768 design, scaling x
///   and y independently, so an aspect ratio other than 4:3 stretches them.
///   The margins do not scale, so on a large display the bar looks tighter.
/// - The repeat rate depends on the push button repeat mechanism and the
///   Win32 millisecond timer (GadgetPushButtonInput()).
///
/// \param listbox The list box that gets the scroll bar; its user data must
///                be a list box record.
///
/// \note Body from Zero Hour's GadgetListboxCreateScrollbar through the BFME 1
/// reconstruction, with BFME 2's one-record creation interface for the child
/// windows. The repeat setup after the creation calls is not in Zero Hour's
/// body as read here.
/// \see GadgetPushButtonInput, GadgetListBoxInput


/// \fn void WindowLayout::addWindow(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Adds a window to the front of the layout's window list, unless it
///        is already there.
///
/// Zero Hour's WindowLayout::load adds each root window that the layout file
/// creates this way, and Zero Hour's GameWindow::winBringToTop removes a
/// window from its layout and adds it again. In BFME 2 the method is virtual,
/// so it is normally reached through the vtable; direct call sites are not
/// the way to find its users. The window becomes the new first window, so a
/// layout lists its windows newest first.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The layout first searches its own list for the window (a linear walk
///   along the windows' next-in-layout links). If the window is found nothing
///   changes.
/// - Otherwise the window's previous link is cleared, its next link is set to
///   the old first window, the old first window's previous link is set to the
///   new one (when there is an old first window), and the first-window pointer
///   moves to the new window. The window is told which layout it is in. The
///   first window ever added also becomes the tail, and the window count goes
///   up by one.
/// - The cost is linear in the number of windows, because of the search.
///
/// \param window The window to add; it must not belong to another layout.
///
/// \warning The function checks only this layout's list. A window that is
/// already in another layout is not removed from it, so that layout's chain
/// is left pointing at a window whose links now belong to this one. Zero Hour
/// asserts that the links are empty before the add; BFME 2's retail code does
/// not. Remove a window from its old layout first.
/// \see WindowLayout::removeWindow, WindowLayout::bringForward


/// \fn void WindowLayout::removeWindow(GameWindow *window)
/// \brief Removes a window from the layout's window list without destroying
///        it.
///
/// Nothing happens if the window is not in this layout. Zero Hour's
/// destroyWindows removes each window this way before asking the window
/// manager to destroy it; code must also remove windows before it deletes a
/// layout, since the layout does not own them.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The layout searches its list for the window; only a member is touched.
/// - The neighbours are linked to each other: the next window's previous link
///   becomes the removed window's previous, and the previous window's next
///   link becomes its next, or, for the first window, the first-window pointer
///   moves to the next window.
/// - The removed window's layout pointer and both links are cleared. If it
///   was the tail, the tail moves to its previous window. The count goes down
///   by one.
///
/// \param window The window to remove.
///
/// \see WindowLayout::addWindow


/// \fn void WindowLayout::bringForward()
/// \brief Raises every window of the layout to the top of the window stack,
///        keeping their relative order.
///
/// The layout-level counterpart of GameWindow::winBringToTop. It is a virtual
/// method, so it is normally reached through the layout's vtable; direct call
/// sites are not the way to find its users.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The loop starts at the tail (the first window added) and walks towards the
/// front, raising each window with GameWindow::winBringToTop. Raising the
/// first-added window first and the newest last leaves the newest on top,
/// which keeps the layout's stacking order. The previous-in-layout pointer
/// is read before each raise because raising a window re-links it (Zero
/// Hour's comment). The loop runs for the layout's window count rather than
/// until a null pointer.
///
/// \warning If the count and the chain disagree the loop would follow a null
/// pointer; Zero Hour has a debug assertion for it that retail lacks.
/// \see WindowLayout::addWindow
